WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.960
Glucose enters and hexokinase traps it as glucose-6 -phosphate, the true branch

2
00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:11.500
point of central metabolism.

3
00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:14.840
From here, the molecule faces a committed choice.

4
00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:21.260
Phospho-glucose isomerase channels it leftward into glycolysis, where the payoff is

5
00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:25.400
ATP and pyruvate, the energy currency the cell spends.

6
00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:31.340
Alternatively, glucose-6 -phosphate dehydrogenase commits it rightward into the

7
00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:37.780
pentose phosphate pathway, whose products are NADPH for reductive biosynthesis and

8
00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:40.720
ribose-5 -phosphate for nucleotide synthesis.

9
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:42.980
The deciding factor is simple.

10
00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.140
What does the cell need right now?

11
00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:47.200
Energy or building blocks?

12
00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:52.420
This fork captures the entire logic of why the pentose phosphate pathway exists

13
00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:53.960
alongside glycolysis.

14
00:00:54.733 --> 00:01:01.133
NAD and NADP look almost identical, differing by a single phosphate, yet the cell

15
00:01:01.133 --> 00:01:05.033
keeps them in dramatically different states for dramatically different purposes.

16
00:01:05.753 --> 00:01:12.073
The NAD pool sits oxidized, roughly a thousand to one in favor of NAD⁺, +, because

17
00:01:12.073 --> 00:01:16.353
catabolism needs an electron acceptor ready to grab electrons from fuel.

18
00:01:16.873 --> 00:01:22.773
The NADP pool is the mirror image, held about ten to one toward NADPH, because

19
00:01:22.773 --> 00:01:26.793
anabolism and antioxidant defense need an electron donor on tap.

20
00:01:27.473 --> 00:01:30.273
Same backbone, opposite redox poise.

21
00:01:30.913 --> 00:01:35.673
By maintaining these as separate cytosolic pools, the cell can run breakdown and

22
00:01:35.673 --> 00:01:40.433
biosynthesis simultaneously without the two electron economies interfering with each

23
00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:41.433
other.

24
00:01:41.433 --> 00:01:46.993
Reactive oxygen species are molecules carrying super-energetic electrons that

25
00:01:46.993 --> 00:01:50.213
attack and damage proteins, lipids, and DNA.

26
00:01:50.953 --> 00:01:55.773
The cell's frontline defense is glutathione, which neutralizes these threats by

27
00:01:55.773 --> 00:01:59.073
taking the oxidative hit so other molecules don't have to.

28
00:01:59.633 --> 00:02:04.633
But once glutathione absorbs that hit, it becomes oxidized and useless, linked into

29
00:02:04.633 --> 00:02:10.973
the GSSG dimer. To get it back into the reduced protective GSH form, the cell spends

30
00:02:10.973 --> 00:02:14.733
NADPH. And where does that NADPH come from?

31
00:02:15.273 --> 00:02:16.873
The pentose phosphate pathway.

32
00:02:17.593 --> 00:02:21.493
So G6PD sits at the very root of antioxidant defense.

33
00:02:21.893 --> 00:02:27.353
Lose that enzyme, lose your ability to regenerate glutathione, and oxidative damage

34
00:02:27.353 --> 00:02:28.353
runs unchecked.

35
00:02:29.067 --> 00:02:33.487
The non-oxidative phase is best understood as carbon bookkeeping carried out by two

36
00:02:33.487 --> 00:02:36.787
enzymes, transketolase and transaldolase.

37
00:02:37.227 --> 00:02:38.227
Track the carbons.

38
00:02:38.707 --> 00:02:43.627
First, transketolase takes two five-carbon sugars and produces a three-carbon and

39
00:02:43.627 --> 00:02:44.827
a seven-carbon sugar.

40
00:02:45.547 --> 00:02:49.947
Then transaldolase converts that three plus seven into a six and a four-carbon

41
00:02:49.947 --> 00:02:56.027
sugar. Finally, transketolase acts again, turning a four and a five into a six and a

42
00:02:56.027 --> 00:02:59.047
three. Some at all and the net result is clean.

43
00:02:59.707 --> 00:03:04.907
Three five-carbon sugars become two six-carbon sugars and one three-carbon sugar.

44
00:03:05.427 --> 00:03:10.487
That is how excess pentoses are recycled back into glycolytic intermediates when the

45
00:03:10.487 --> 00:03:12.367
cell needs energy more than ribose.

46
00:03:13.167 --> 00:03:18.967
The pentose phosphate pathway is not one fixed route, but four operating modes, and

47
00:03:18.967 --> 00:03:21.827
the cell selects among them based on what it needs.

48
00:03:22.467 --> 00:03:28.067
When it needs ribose for nucleotides but little NADPH, it runs only the non

49
00:03:28.067 --> 00:03:33.927
-oxidative phase. When it needs both NADPH and ribose imbalance, it runs the

50
00:03:33.927 --> 00:03:35.527
oxidative phase straight through.

51
00:03:36.167 --> 00:03:41.827
When it needs lots of NADPH but little ribose, it routes ribose back into glycolytic

52
00:03:41.827 --> 00:03:43.667
intermediates and recycles them.

53
00:03:44.287 --> 00:03:49.547
And when it needs NADPH plus ATP, it pushes carbon all the way into pyruvate.

54
00:03:50.027 --> 00:03:53.967
One pathway, four behaviors, each a different combination of glycolysis,

55
00:03:54.127 --> 00:03:55.707
gluconeogenesis, and the shunt.
